- exec-redirection-and-error-broken: narrowed down to one failure in
  `exec-p.tst` (9/10, not 7/10 as previously logged here — the other
  two entries this bullet used to cite were fixed as a side effect of
  an unrelated change earlier in the same session that produced
  fixes/184/185). The remaining failure is `exec-p.tst:15`, "exec
  with redirections":

  ```sh
  exec >&2 2>/dev/null
  echo reached
  ```

  must send "reached" to stderr (fd 1 was dup'd from fd 2 *before* fd
  2 got retargeted to /dev/null); shish sends it to the original
  stdout instead.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  sh tests/run-tst.sh build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish tests/posix exec-p.tst
  # 1 failure: exec-p.tst:15
  ```

  **Root cause (confirmed):** `fd_dup()` (`src/fd/fd_dup.c`), used for
  every `[n]>&m` redirection including persistent ("exec") ones, does
  NOT take an independent copy of the source fd's state — it copies
  *pointers* into the source struct's own embedded buffer storage
  (`d->r = dupe->r; d->w = dupe->w; d->dup = dupe;`) and leaves the
  real kernel dup2() to happen lazily later (`fdtable_dup()`,
  triggered by `fdtable_exec()`/`fdtable_lazy()`). For `exec >&2
  2>/dev/null`, by the time that lazy resolution actually runs, the
  *second* redirection has already run `fd_new(2,...)` ->
  `fdtable_newfd(2,...)` -> `fd_reinit()`, which reuses/reinitializes
  the exact same `struct fd` that fd 1's still-pending dup is chasing
  via `->dup`. The pending dup then resolves against the *new*
  (/dev/null) occupant instead of the original fd-2 target.

  **What I tried (session ending 2026-08-17), and why it's reverted:**
  Tried forcing the real dup2() to happen eagerly, inside
  `redir_dup()`, right after `fd_dup()` sets up the pending dup, so it
  captures the source before any later redirection in the same list
  can reinit its struct. Needed `fdtable_dup(nredir->fd,
  FDTABLE_FORCE | FDTABLE_CLOSE)` specifically (plain
  `fdtable_resolve()`/`FDTABLE_MOVE` alone hits a self-referential
  "redirection cycle detected" error, because the fd being resolved
  is already linked into its own fdtable slot, and going through
  `fdtable_wish()`/`fdtable_gap()` has it try to relocate itself out
  of its own way; `FDTABLE_CLOSE` skips that. `FDTABLE_FORCE` is
  needed too because fds 0-2 used to never actually get `close()`d
  when torn down — see the fd≤2-guard writeup below, fixes/186 — so
  fdtable's bookkeeping believed slot 1 was free while the kernel fd
  was still open, and only `FDTABLE_FORCE`'s dup2()-retry path could
  actually reclaim it).

  This made `exec-p.tst:15` and the simple repro above pass. **But it
  introduced a reliably reproducible segfault** on a previously-working
  pattern (the classic stdout/stderr swap idiom, `tests/fixed.sh`
  fixes/187's test). That crash is now understood and fixed
  separately (fixes/187, `fd_state_save()`/`fd_state_restore()` around
  `eval_subshell.c`'s existing `fdstack_push()`/`fdstack_pop()` pair) —
  see "TODO.md, Goal 4" for the full writeup. So the segfault is no
  longer a reason not to retry the eager-dup2() approach above; it
  should just also carry the `fd_state_save`/`fd_state_restore` fix
  (or run after it, since that fix is already in the tree).

  All three changed files (`src/redir.h`, `src/redir/redir_eval.c`,
  `src/redir/redir_dup.c`) were reverted back to their original state
  before ending the session — the tree currently has NO changes
  related to this specific bug (`src/redir/redir_dup.c`'s original
  signature is `int redir_dup(struct nredir* nredir, stralloc* sa)`,
  2 params, no `persistent` flag). What *did* land this session,
  discovered while chasing the segfault above: the fd≤2 `close()`
  guard that was masking a family of real fd-table bookkeeping bugs
  got removed for good (fixes/186), and the subshell fd-bookkeeping
  scoping gap that caused the segfault got fixed (fixes/187) — both
  documented in `TODO.md`'s Goal 4 section. Retrying the eager-dup2()
  fix for *this* bug now starts from firmer ground.

  **Repro for the (now-fixed) crash**, kept here since it's still a
  useful regression check for whoever retries the eager-dup2() fix:
  ```sh
  build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish -c '
  X=$(mktemp); Y=$(mktemp)
  ( exec 3>&1 1>&2 2>&3 3>&-; echo a; echo b >&2 ) > "$X" 2> "$Y"
  echo "exit=$?"; cat "$X"; cat "$Y"
  rm -f "$X" "$Y"
  '
  # must not crash; must print exit=0 / a on stderr's file / b on stdout's file
  ```

