  ```

- grouping-p-tst-flaky: `grouping-p.tst:34` ("subshell ending with
  asynchronous list", `(echo foo >fifo1&); cat fifo1`) used to hang
  outright (fixed alongside `fixes/103`, the general async-list fix
  -- this was the same missing-/dev/null-default bug, just reached
  through a subshell's own backgrounded list instead of a top-level
  one). It now passes most runs, but still intermittently fails
  (roughly 1 run in 5, `grouping-p.tst: 11/12 passed`) -- some
  remaining race between the subshell's background writer and the
  FIFO read that follows it, not yet root-caused.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  # run several times; fails intermittently, not on every run
  sh tests/run-tst.sh build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish tests/posix grouping-p.tst
  ```

- test-binary-and-or-segfault: `test`/`[` segfaults on the 3-argument
  form when the middle argument is `-a`/`-o` and the operands are
  plain strings. `test -n x -a -n y` (5 arguments) is fine, so the
  crash is in how the 3-argument form is dispatched -- the fix is
  POSIX's argument-*count*-first algorithm (XCU 4.62), not more
  operator sniffing.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  echo 'test 1 -a 1' > t.sh && shish t.sh; echo $?   # 139 (SIGSEGV)
  # same for: test 1 -o 1 / test "" -a ""
  ```

- error-message-line-number-off-by-one: diagnostics report the line
  the parser has advanced to, not the line of the command that
  failed, and omit the offending name. `$LINENO` itself is correct,
  so this is the message only.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  printf 'echo one\necho ${x?boom}\n' > e.sh && shish e.sh
  # shish: e.sh:3:1: boom      (bash: e.sh: line 2: x: boom)
  ```

- fixed-sh-assumes-optional-builtins: `tests/fixed.sh` fails 3
  assertions on a default build because it assumes the optional `cat`
  and `rm` builtins are compiled in (`BUILTIN_CAT`/`BUILTIN_RM` are 0
  by default): the `cat -n` case compares against shish's own
  builtin's column format, and two `set +h` cases run `rm` with a
  PATH that only contains the test's own directories. Either gate
  those cases on the builtin being present or stop depending on it.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  ./build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish tests/fixed.sh | grep FAIL
  ```
